-- title:
--   An eventual upper bound for the logarithmic Taylor order
-- statement:
--   Write \(a_n=\log n/\log\log n\) and \(L_C(n)=\lceil Ca_n\rceil_+\),
--   where \(\lceil x\rceil_+=\max\{0,\lceil x\rceil\}\). For every fixed \(C>0\), all sufficiently large integers \(n\) satisfy
--   \[
--   L_C(n)\le2Ca_n.
--   \]
--   Thus taking an integer Taylor order by upward rounding increases the intended order by at most a factor of two eventually.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/hanshengjiang/reweighted-npmle-lean/blob/5af8d16822c5a8a3f032b13eb95daea40f307484/ReweightedNPMLE/GaussianNearMLERate.lean#L88-L103

